Read `AGENTS.md` at the clone root if it exists. Where it and this brief disagree, this brief wins.

# Brief

## GOAL
Task type: {{.Type}}. The issue, verbatim:

### {{.Title}}

{{.Body}}

## SCOPE
- Edit project files only inside this clone, your working directory.
- Read, search and run only inside this clone; you may also read and write your scratch directory below.
  Never access the repository around it, the rest of `.atm/runs/` or another clone.
- No network, except the project's package manager installing a dependency.
- Your scratch directory, outside the clone, is `{{.Scratch}}`, your `$TMPDIR`: logs, outputs and files you set
  aside go there, never in the system temp directory.
- Test files are those matching {{code .TestPatterns}}. Docs files are those matching {{code .DocsPatterns}}.
  Every other file is source.

## ACCEPTANCE
{{- if eq .Type "fix"}}
1. Write a test on the existing code that fails today on behaviour, not on a missing symbol or module.
   The fix may add files: the test must still fail with those files present and the fix reverted.
   ATM runs the red test with your source changes reverted to the base commit, so the test may only name
   identifiers that already exist there; reach new behaviour at runtime through an existing entry point, and
   check it by building or vetting with only the test file changed.
2. Fix the source. The test passes.
{{- else if eq .Type "feature"}}
1. Write a test that fails today, possibly on a missing symbol at runtime. It must parse/compile and reach
   an assertion: a parse, compile or load error before any assertion is not a red.
   ATM runs the red test with your source changes reverted to the base commit, so the test may only name
   identifiers that already exist there; reach new behaviour at runtime through an existing entry point, and
   check it by building or vetting with only the test file changed.
2. Implement the feature. The test passes.
{{- else if eq .Type "greenfield"}}
1. Write a test that fails today, possibly on a missing module at runtime. It must parse/compile and reach
   an assertion: a parse, compile or load error before any assertion is not a red.
2. Build it. The test passes.
{{- else if eq .Type "refactor"}}
1. No red test: the full suite passes before and after the change.
2. Do not touch existing tests.
{{- else if eq .Type "tests"}}
1. Write the new test. It passes on the base commit and after your change.
2. Do not touch source.
{{- else if eq .Type "docs"}}
1. Change only docs: do not touch source or tests.
2. The project's commands below pass.
{{- else if eq .Type "chore"}}
1. Reinstall dependencies{{with .Install}} (`{{.}}`){{end}} after your change.
2. The project's commands below pass.
{{- end}}
3. Then every command in VERIFY passes.

## VERIFY
- Test: the red test, through {{cmd .TestFile}}, and the tests of each package you changed; not the whole
  suite, which ATM runs after you.
- Typecheck: {{cmd .Typecheck}}
- Lint: {{cmd .Lint}}

## STYLE
Use the `ponytail` skill.

## FORBIDDEN
- Anything the issue does not ask for. A real behaviour gap you see and do not fix goes in your report's
  summary, as a note.
- `git commit`, `git push`, `git rebase`, any `gh` command.
- `sleep` in tests.

## REPORT
Your final message must be ONLY this JSON object, nothing else:
{"test_file": "<repo-relative path of the new test, or empty>", "changed_files": ["<path>"], "summary": "<one paragraph>", "commands_run": ["<command>"]}
